'''''Primula'' × ''polyantha''''', the '''polyanthus primrose''' or '''false oxlip''', is a naturally occurring [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] species of flowering plant in the family [[Primulaceae]].<ref name="702440-1" >{{cite POWO |id=702440-1 |title=''Primula'' × ''polyantha'' Mill. |access-date=13 April 2022 }}</ref><ref name="natural" >{{cite web |url=https://www.naturespot.org.uk/species/false-oxlip |title=False Oxlip - ''Primula vulgaris x veris = P. x polyantha'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=naturespot.org.uk |publisher=NatureSpot |access-date=13 April 2022 }}</ref> It is the result of crosses between ''[[Primula veris]]'' (common cowslip) and ''[[Primula vulgaris]]'' (common primrose).<ref name="PRIPO" >{{cite web |url=https://gd.eppo.int/taxon/PRIPO |title=Primula Polyanthus hybrids (PRIPO) |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2022 |website=EPPO Global Database |publisher=European and Mediterranean Plant Protection Organization |access-date=13 April 2022 }}</ref> It is native to Europe, found where the parent species' ranges overlap, and many artificial hybrid cultivars have also been created for the garden trade.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Recurrent hybridisation events between ''Primula vulgaris'', ''P. veris'' and ''[[Primula elatior|P. elatior]]'' (Primulaceae, Ericales) challenge the species boundaries: Using molecular markers to re-evaluate morphological identifications |year=2018 |last1=Tendal |first1=Kira |last2=Ørgaard |first2=Marian |last3=Larsen |first3=Bjarne |last4=Pedersen |first4=Carsten |journal=Nordic Journal of Botany |volume=36 |issue=9 |article-number=e01778 |doi=10.1111/njb.01778 |s2cid=89708128 }}</ref><ref name="MBG_265522" >{{cite web |url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=265522 |title=''Primula'' (polyanthus type) |author=<!--Not stated--> |date= |website=Plant Finder |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden |access-date=13 April 2022 }}</ref> Naturally{{nbh}}occurring individuals (the false oxlips) tend to have yellow flowers, while a wide range of flower colors has been developed in the cultivars (the polyanthus primroses) over the centuries.<ref name="natural" /><ref name="MBG_265522" />
